<title>LATVIA: CENTRAL BANK RANKS DEPOSIT TAKERS.</title>
<headline>CENTRAL BANK RANKS DEPOSIT TAKERS.</headline>
<dateline>RIGA 1997-05-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>The central bank on Friday said that</p>
<p>16 Latvian banks have qualified to accept deposits from</p>
<p>individuals.</p>
<p>Following a severe banking crises which hit the country in</p>
<p>1995, a new law on credit institutions demands a special licence</p>
<p>from the central bank since early last year to allow banks to</p>
<p>accept deposits.</p>
<p>Two years of international audit and a minimum of one</p>
<p>million lats of capital and reserves are among the conditions.</p>
<p>The bank gave a list of the banks ranking them according to</p>
<p>capital and reserves by March 31.</p>
<p>    BANK		   CAPITAL AND RESERVES</p>
<p>			    (in thousands of lats)</p>
<p>    Unibanka			 27.464</p>
<p>    Parekss Bank		   24.156</p>
<p>    Rigas komercbanka	    13.003</p>
<p>    Rietumu Bank		   11.400</p>
<p>    Baltic Transit Bank	   8.008</p>
<p>    Zemes Bank			7.145</p>
<p>    Saules Bank		     5.435</p>
<p>    Savings Bank		    3.782</p>
<p>    Maras Bank			3.590</p>
<p>    Hansabank-Latvija	     3.573</p>
<p>    Mortgage and Land Bank	2.942</p>
<p>    Trasta Bank		     2.842</p>
<p>    Latvijas kapital-banka	2.632</p>
<p>    Multibank			 2.369</p>
<p>Figures for Societe Generale Riga branch were not given.</p>
<p>The Latvian Investment Bank is also a full licence holder.</p>
<p>-- Martinsh Gravitis, Riga Newsroom, +371 721 5240</p>
</text>
